    "## **1. Introduction**\n",
    "\n",
    "In January 2026, Estonia’s electricity system experienced a severe stress event. ERR reported record-high January prices, with monthly averages around **154 €/MWh** and hourly peaks approaching **500 €/MWh**[cite: 3]. The main drivers were unusually cold weather, weak wind generation, and outages at major production units, exposing a narrow resilience margin[cite: 3].\n",
    "\n",
    "This project treats the event as a resilience problem—defined as the capacity of a system to withstand, adapt to, and recover from disruption[cite: 2]. Following the 2025 disconnection from the Russian **BRELL** grid, Estonia’s stability relies on domestic generation and regional interconnections like **EstLink 1 and 2**[cite: 3].\n",
    "\n",
    "We specifically investigate if planned wind farm expansions could have materially improved resilience during this crisis. This shifts the focus from decarbonization to **Strategic Autonomy**, evaluating system performance during the \"worst hours\" rather than average conditions[cite: 2, 3]."
